\description{
Fits a Pella-Tomlinson surplus production model to the catch and one or
more relative abundance indices in a \link[=data-class]{data} object, and returns
estimates of stock status and MSY-based reference points. The fitted model
is used by the \code{\link[=SurplusProduction]{SurplusProduction()}} management procedure, and \code{FitSP()}
can also be used directly on real or simulated \code{Data} objects.
}
\details{
\subsection{Model}{

Biomass follows
\deqn{\frac{dB}{dt} = \gamma \, MSY \left[\frac{B}{K} - \left(\frac{B}{K}\right)^n\right] - F B,
  \qquad \gamma = \frac{n^{n/(n-1)}}{n-1},}{dB/dt = gamma MSY [B/K - (B/K)^n] - F B,  gamma = n^(n/(n-1)) / (n-1),}
where \code{n} is the shape parameter (\code{Shape}; \code{2} is the Schaefer model, and
\code{n = 1} the Fox model, used when \code{Shape} is within \code{FoxTol} of \code{1}; see
\code{\link[=SPControl]{SPControl()}}). The estimated parameters are \code{FMSY} and \code{MSY}, and
optionally the initial depletion (\code{B/K} at the start of the first fitted
year, \code{Depletion}) and \code{Shape}. Carrying capacity is
\code{K = MSY / (FMSY * BMSY/K)} with \code{BMSY/K = n^(1/(1-n))}.

The model is deterministic (no process error) and the catch is assumed
known. The fishing mortality in each year is solved so that the predicted
catch equals the observed catch. Each year is integrated in \code{nSubStep}
sub-steps (see \code{\link[=SPControl]{SPControl()}}).

Each index is assumed proportional to biomass at the index's \code{Timing}
within the year, with lognormal observation error. The catchability of
each index is calculated analytically at its maximum likelihood value.
The observation standard deviation is either estimated for each index
(\code{IndexSD = 'estimate'}, with lower bound \code{MinSD}, see \code{\link[=SPControl]{SPControl()}}) or
calculated from the index \code{CV} in \code{Data} (\code{IndexSD = 'data'},
\code{sd = sqrt(log(1 + CV^2))}). The negative log-likelihood of each index is
multiplied by its \code{IndexWeight}.

For seasonal data (\code{Data@Seasons > 1}), \code{Data} is first aggregated to
calendar years with \code{\link[=AnnualData]{AnnualData()}}. The timing of an aggregated index is
the mean timing, as a fraction of the calendar year, of the seasons in
\code{IndexSeasons}.
}

\subsection{Models}{
\itemize{
\item \code{Model = 'internal'} (default): the model above, fitted by maximum
likelihood with \code{\link[stats:nlminb]{stats::nlminb()}}. Numerical settings are set with
\code{\link[=SPControl]{SPControl()}}.
\item \code{Model = 'spict'}: the stochastic surplus production model in
continuous time (SPiCT; Pedersen and Berg 2017), fitted with the \code{spict}
package, which must be installed (see \code{\link[=SpictControl]{SpictControl()}}). SPiCT estimates
process error and observation error in the catch as well as the
indices. The \code{FitSP()} arguments are converted to \code{spict} inputs as
described in \code{\link[=SpictControl]{SpictControl()}}.
}
}
